       {
        "name": "Market Approach",
        "children": [
         {
          "name": "Guideline public company method",
          "children": [
           {
            "name": "In private company valuation as has been noted, valuation based on multiples of similar public companies is often referred to as the guideline public company method (GPCM)"
           },
           {
            "name": "Adjust for the differences in leverage between private companies and comparable public companies.",
            "children": [
             {
              "name": "Step 1: “unlever” observed public company beta"
             },
             {
              "name": "Step 2: derive a levered beta"
             }
            ]
           },
           {
            "name": "To estimate a control premium",
            "children": [
             {
              "name": "Type of transactionType of transaction: compared with financial transactions, control premiums for an acquisition by a strategic buyer are typically larger"
             },
             {
              "name": "Industry factorsIndustry factors: pricing of public companies in the sector may reflect some part of a possible control premium in the share prices"
             },
             {
              "name": "Type of considerationType of consideration: transactions involving the exchange of significant amounts of stock might be less relevant as a basis of measuring a control premium because of the possibility that acquiring companies time such transactions during periods when their management perceives that shares of their company are overvalued in the marketplace"
             },
             {
              "name": "The multiple resulting from applying a control premium to pricing multiples from publicly-traded companies should be assessed for reasonableness"
             }
            ]
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "name": "Guideline transactions method",
          "children": [
           {
            "name": "The GTM uses pricing multiples derived from acquisitions of public or private companies. Transaction multiples would be the most relevant evidence for valuation of a controlling interest in a private company"
           },
           {
            "name": "A number of factors need to be considered in assessing transaction-based pricing multiples",
            "children": [
             {
              "name": "Synergies: the pricing of strategic acquisitions may include payment for anticipated synergies"
             },
             {
              "name": "Contingent consideration: contingent consideration represents potential future payments to the seller that are contingent on the achievement of certain agreed on occurrences"
             },
             {
              "name": "Non-cash consideration: acquisitions may include stock in the consideration. The cash equivalent value of a large block of stock may create uncertainty regarding the transaction price"
             },
             {
              "name": "Availability of transactions: Meaningful transactions for a specific private company may be limited. The relevance of pricing indications from a historical transaction may be challenged given any significant changes to the company, industry, or economy over the period."
             },
             {
              "name": "Changes between transaction date and valuation date: Changes in market conditions could result in different risk and growth expectations, requiring an adjustment to the pricing multiple."
             },
             {
              "name": "Differences in company size, country, tax status, and leverage may also be relevant."
             }
            ]
           },
